          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What kind of physical activity is described for people with type 2 diabetes?",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nThe guidance describes two main kinds of activity for type 2 diabetes:\n\n- **Resistance training** (exercises that work muscles against weight or resistance). It improves blood sugar control and helps keep muscle, which matters more with age and during weight loss. The guidance describes it alongside aerobic activity, not as a replacement, and the combination works better than either alone. (*Physical activity for type 2 diabetes*)\n- **Breaking up long periods of sitting.** Standing up and moving lightly every 30 minutes is described as improving blood sugar control. This is a separate recommendation from the weekly total, and it may be easier to fit into a desk-based day. (*Physical activity for type 2 diabetes*)\n\nThe guidance also notes that activity can lower blood sugar even when body weight does not change. (*Physical activity for type 2 diabetes*)\n\nSome complications change what is advised. The retrieved text says that vigorous or high-impact activity may need assessment first in some cases, such as possible heart involvement or certain eye problems. Foot problems can make cycling or swimming preferable to weight-bearing activity. Activity is also put off when blood sugar is very high with ketones. (*Physical activity for type 2 diabetes*) Anyone with these concerns should talk with their clinician before starting or changing an exercise plan.\n\nThe search results did not give a diabetes-specific weekly amount of aerobic activity, so I can't tell you how much to do from these sources. A clinician or diabetes educator can help set this based on your situation.",
